      "flags": {},
      "order": 29,
      "mode": 0,
      "title": "Latent only, no conditioning",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "### Plain latent upscale\n\nNo conditioning here -- just the AV latent, split into video/audio, the video stream upscaled through the neural backbone, recombined. Ordinary LTXVSeparateAVLatent -> upscaler -> LTXVConcatAVLatent, same as the sibling latent-upscaler pack's own examples use."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 30,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        900,
        1620
      ],
      "size": [
        400,
        260
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 30,
      "mode": 0,
      "title": "From-source rebuild",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "### The `latent` input is what does it\n\nSame timeline, same prompt as Conditioning A -- but with the upscaled latent connected to `latent`, this node reads its canvas from THAT instead of the resolution/aspect widgets (which are ignored once latent is connected), and re-encodes every keyframe from its ORIGINAL source file at the new canvas. Re-runs the full text encoder too -- the cost of this path vs. MiniMax H3 Timeline Latent Upscale's cheaper reuse of the already-encoded keyframe latents."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 31,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        900,
        1000
      ],
      "size": [
        380,
        200
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 31,
      "mode": 0,
      "title": "The other path",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "### Compare with the matched-pair workflow\n\nSee MiniMaxH3_Timeline_TwoPass_LatentUpscale.json for the cheaper alternative: one MiniMax H3 Timeline Latent Upscale node upscales the already-encoded keyframe latents instead of rebuilding conditioning from source -- no second CLIP/text-encoder pass, one Conditioning node total instead of two."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
